The '''Southern Kaftian Tradition''' (also known as the '''Buran-Haro Complex''') is an {{W|archaeological horizon}} encompassing the diverse [[Burano-Ormaic languages|Burano-Ormaic]]-speaking cultures that developed along the southern coast and highlands of [[Southwestern Kaftia]] prior to the formation of the [[Burawa|Burawi Kingdom]]. Distinguished from the earthen tumuli of the [[Northern Kaftian Tradition|Northern Tradition]] and the megalithic circles of the [[Western Kaftian Tradition|Western Tradition]], it is characterised by dry-stone masonry, the early development of indigenous iron metallurgy, and the emergence of river-valley chiefdoms that would evolve into the classical kingdoms of the [[Ancient Southwestern Kaftia|Ancient Era]].

== Geography and cultural zones ==

The Southern Tradition is divided into three geopolitical zones, each defined by the drainage basin of one or more rivers flowing from the [[Great Kaftian Mountain Range]].

=== Western lowlands ===
The western zone (modern western [[Burawa]]) is characterised by broad, rolling plains and fertile river valleys fed by the [[Hooyal River|Hooyal]] and [[Dafar River|Dafar]] rivers. Protected to the northwest by the [[Kurinka Hills]], which separate the region from the [[Western Kaftian Tradition]], the inhabitants developed a productive agricultural economy. The surplus grain produced by these communities supported high population densities, and the region became known in later periods as the site of the [[Kingdom of Sago]].

=== Central cove ===
The central zone is drained by the steep, fast-flowing [[Galbeed River]], which drops rapidly from the highlands to the [[Mesogean Sea]], creating a sheltered cove-like embayment. The communities of this region acted as intermediaries, moving goods between the grain-producing west and the mineral-rich east. The city of [[Samara]], which later became a major trading hub, grew from this tradition of middleman commerce.

=== Eastern complex ===
The eastern zone, dominated by the [[Buran River]] system and the [[Lake Haro]] basin, was the most politically complex region of the Southern Tradition. The Upper Buran valley, high in the [[Kushish]] highlands, was the heartland of the [[Ufyen]] culture, whose chieftains controlled the region's iron deposits and the mountain passes leading north. The [[Lake Haro]] basin, straddling the modern [[Burawa]]/[[Maianka]] border, supported a distinct lacustrine culture known for its "Black Ware" pottery and fishing economy. At the river mouths on the [[Mesogean Sea]] coast, proto-urban centres such as [[Mazar]] and [[Iskhal]] developed, growing wealthy from maritime trade.

== Archaeological features ==

=== Rock art ===
The earliest evidence of the Southern Tradition is the Rock Art Complex of the [[Kushish]] and [[Orma]] highlands (~10,000–5,000 BCE). These paintings and engravings, found in rock shelters throughout the highlands, depict hunting scenes involving the now-extinct '''{{W|African forest elephant|Mesogean pygmy elephant}}''', as well as geometric patterns and handprints. The pygmy elephant was driven to extinction by the combined effects of hunting pressure and habitat loss as terraced agriculture expanded into its range during the Bronze and Iron Ages.

=== Iron metallurgy ===
[[File:Ufyen_Iron_Smelting.jpg|thumb|right|Artist's reconstruction of an early iron smelting furnace in the Upper [[Buran River]] valley (~1,000 BCE).]]
Around 1,000 BCE, metalworkers in the Upper [[Buran River]] valley developed the technique of smelting {{W|iron ore}} from the abundant deposits of the [[Kushish]] highlands. The introduction of iron tools had two principal consequences: iron axes enabled the clearing of dense {{W|maquis}} and highland forest, expanding the area available for agriculture; and iron weapons gave the highland chiefdoms a decisive military advantage over lowland communities that still relied on bronze or stone, enabling the establishment of the first tributary polities in the region.

=== Settlement patterns ===
[[File:Southern_Hillfort_Kaas.jpg|thumb|left|Ruins of a dry-stone hillfort (''kaas'') in the [[Kushish]] highlands, with walls up to 4 metres thick.]]
In the highlands, communities constructed fortified hilltop settlements known as ''kaas'', using dry-stone techniques to build defensive walls up to 4 metres thick on ridges and promontories. These sites served as both residences for the ruling elite and as defensible storage centres for grain and metal. On the coast, by contrast, settlements were typically open and grid-planned, centred on a market square and a temple complex.

== Economy and trade ==

The economy of the Southern Tradition was driven by exchange between the highlands and the coast. The highlands produced iron tools, copper, tin, and timber for shipbuilding, while the lowlands supplied grain, olive oil, dried fish, and salt.

=== Mesogean trade ===
[[File:Southern_Coastal_Trade.jpg|thumb|right|Artist's reconstruction of the port of [[Mazar]] at the mouth of the [[Buran River]], circa 400 BCE.]]
By approximately 600 BCE, the coastal cities of [[Mazar]], [[Iskhal]], and [[Sabia]] had established commercial contact with maritime civilisations on the southern shore of the [[Mesogean Sea]]. This trade network brought gold and silver — metals scarce on the peninsula — in exchange for Kaftian iron and copper. The resulting influx of foreign wealth widened the economic gap between the highland chieftains who controlled the mining regions and the coastal elites who managed the export trade, a tension that defined the political history of the region for the following millennium.

== Religion ==

The Southern Tradition's religious practices were organised around a hierarchical pantheon. '''[[Waaq]]''', the Sky Father, was the supreme deity, worshipped on mountain peaks such as [[Buur Waaq]] and associated with rain, justice, and royal authority. In the agricultural lowlands, an '''Earth Mother''' figure was venerated as the source of soil fertility and the guarantor of the harvest. Around [[Lake Haro]], a localised cult of the '''Lake Spirit''' persisted, distinct from the main pantheon and sharing certain features with the Lake Spirit worship of the [[Northern Kaftian Tradition|Northern Tradition]].

== Legacy ==

The Southern Kaftian Tradition is the direct ancestor of the classical [[Burawa|Burawi civilisation]]. The long-standing tension between the highland [[Ufyen]] chieftains and the coastal trading cities was resolved in 403 AD with the formation of the [[Mazar-Iskhal alliance]], which created a unified political entity along the southern coast and set the stage for the medieval [[Burawa|Burawi Kingdom]].

